  "summary": "Four candidate studies directly apply transformer-style models (self-attention encoder models) to energy demand forecasting, operationalised as predicting electricity consumption. The strongest match is [V0126], which evaluates self-attention encoder models for predicting electricity consumption on the ProtMap dataset with a test MAE of 0.93 — a regression metric well-suited to demand forecasting. [V0125] applies the same method to electricity consumption prediction on GridWatch, reporting accuracy of 0.86. [V0127] targets electricity consumption with self-attention encoder models on FireScope but reports a weaker AUROC of 0.65. [V0128] is an older deployment study comparing baselines against self-attention encoder models for electricity consumption prediction on ChurnBench, with F1 = 0.72. Only these four documents jointly satisfy the requested method (transformers / self-attention encoder models) and topic (energy demand / electricity consumption forecasting); other electricity-consumption papers use non-transformer methods (e.g., message passing in [V0124], decentralised training in [V0131]) and are therefore excluded. To complete the five-item ranking, [V0077] is included as the closest adjacent work: it applies the same transformer method to an energy-domain estimation task (battery remaining life on CellLife, AUROC 0.87), though it does not address demand forecasting directly.",
